## 3. Tool surface (one registry, two transports)

`app/models/agent/tools/*` each subclasses `Agent::Tool` and declares, with the `tool` macro, a `description`, `input` properties plus a `required` list, an `output` schema (every key required unless listed in `output_optional`, `additionalProperties: false` on both schemas), `call(**args)`, and permission metadata: `read_only`, `mcp`, `scheduled`. The tool's wire name is derived from the class name (`GetPortfolio` is `get_portfolio`). The MCP server registers tools where `mcp`; the scheduled runtime passes only tools where `scheduled && read_only`, enforced in one place (and at class-definition time: an unattended tool that is not read-only fails eager load) so a Phase 2 write tool cannot reach an unattended run by omission. `Agent::Tools.call(name, arguments, transport:)` is the one entry point for both transports: it validates the arguments against the input schema with the gem's validator (so the scheduled runtime gets the same gate the MCP gem applies), invokes the tool, normalizes every failure into a `Result` (`value`, `error`, `retry_after`, `ok?`, `text`), and writes the `tool_calls` row for every call including a denied one (an unknown tool and a tool the transport may not use are recorded and reported differently). Tools raise `Agent::Tool::InvalidArguments` for what the schema cannot express; any other exception is reported under the tool's name and returned as a tool error.

| Tool | Returns |
|---|---|
| `get_portfolio` | Positions with last price, unrealized P&L, working stop, distance to stop, per-position open risk, `unprotected` flag, synced_at per row |
| `get_account_health` | Equity, settled/unsettled cash, drawdown from HWM (index-based), unprotected count, token status and age, last sync per feed, shadow mode, halt state, sync pause reason |
| `get_quotes(symbols)` | Schwab `/marketdata/v1/quotes`, cached 2 min in Solid Cache, each quote stamped with fetched_at; when the token is expired returns the cached value with `stale: true` or an explicit unavailable error |
| `get_price_history(symbol, range)` | Schwab `/marketdata/v1/pricehistory`, cached 5 min |
| `get_journal(filters)` | Trades, legs on request; filters: status, needs_review, setup, symbol, opened/closed after, missing_post_mortem; `limit` 50 default, 100 max (200 with legs, sent twice, would pass the Claude app's result cap) |
| `get_setup_stats` | Stored stats from `setups` |
| `get_risk_rules` | `available: false, rules: []` in Phase 1; reads no rows until the guardrail engine exists |

Every call writes a `tool_calls` row. Nothing in this section writes to `audit_events`.

**MCP server (revised after the step 8 counselors review).** `McpController < ActionController::API` at `match "/mcp", via: [post, get, delete]`: a `before_action` checks the bearer (`Bearer` scheme only, case-insensitive; `secure_compare`; never logged) and answers 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"` and a one-line JSON body, no HTML page and no `resource_metadata` (that would send Claude looking for an authorization server that does not exist). The action builds `MCP::Server` from the registry and hands the request to the gem's `StreamableHTTPTransport` with `stateless: true`, `enable_json_response: true`, `serve_subscriptions_listen: false`, `dns_rebinding_protection: false` (Rails host authorization owns the Host check; feeding the gem `request.host` would have it match the header against itself), and `max_request_bytes` well under the 4 MiB default (Rails parses the JSON body for its log line before the gem's cap runs, so the nginx `client_max_body_size` in §9 is the real first guard); the transport handles GET, DELETE, the protocol-version header, notifications (202), and both the 2025 handshake and the 2026-07-28 stateless transport. Rails `rate_limit to: 60, within: 1.minute` on the controller; JSON only; no CORS. The server advertises only the `tools` capability. Tools return `MCP::Tool::Response` with `structured_content` plus a text block of the same JSON, `additionalProperties: false` and `required` on every input and output schema, `validate_tool_call_results` on outside production so schema drift fails a spec rather than a Claude session, a failed call carries `retry_after` in `_meta`, and annotations `read_only_hint: true, destructive_hint: false, idempotent_hint: true, open_world_hint: false`. Tool exceptions never reach the transport: one dispatch wrapper rescues, writes the `tool_calls` row in an `ensure`, and returns a tool error carrying only the Schwab error message. Quotes and price history are cached with a long expiry and their own `fetched_at`; age beyond the freshness window triggers a refetch and a failed refetch returns the old entry with `stale: true`; the tools draw on a 70/min ceiling of the shared Schwab budget (passed as `budget:` to `Schwab.client`, taken once per request inside the client) so a chatty session cannot starve sync, and a rate limit is a tool error with `retry_after`, never a sleep. `get_portfolio` and `get_account_health` wrap the `Dashboard` read model rather than re-deriving anything; outputs carry the `protection` enum, nil for unknown risk, decimal strings for money, ISO 8601 New York timestamps, and never a `raw` column, an account hash, or the user's email (accounts are labelled by role). `get_journal` paginates (`limit` 50 default, 200 max, `offset`, `include_legs` off by default); `get_quotes` takes at most 50 symbols, reports `invalid_symbols` and `unavailable_symbols` (no fetch and nothing cached), answers only-invalid symbols as data rather than an outage, and raises the Schwab error only when nothing cached can stand in; `get_price_history` takes an enum range mapped to daily or weekly candles; `get_risk_rules` returns an empty list with `available: false` so the agent does not infer missing data. A spec walks every tool's serialized output for the account-hash pattern and `accountNumber`.

**Connector auth.** Ship bearer-only first: in the Claude connector dialog choose Authentication "None" and add an `authorization` request header whose value is `Bearer <token>` verbatim (the headers section is an org-gated beta; header values cannot be edited after adding, so rotating the token means removing and re-adding the connector). If the dialog steers to OAuth after probing the URL, the fallback within step 8 is lazy gating: `initialize` and `tools/list` unauthenticated, the bearer required on `tools/call`. The real OAuth path (protected-resource metadata at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp`, authorization-server metadata, S256 PKCE, a pre-registered client id, the `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback` redirect, a form-encoded token endpoint, refresh rotation, audience validation) is its own build step if the headers beta turns out to be unavailable, not a footnote to this one. Claude Code can drive the same server with `claude mcp add --transport http --header` for verification either way.

**Scheduled runtime (revised after the step 9 counselors review).** `Agent::Run` wraps one Messages API conversation with `claude-sonnet-5` (one constant, stored per run; Opus costs 2.5x for synthesis over structured data) through the `anthropic` gem with a 120-second request timeout and two retries, the key passed explicitly from credentials, never the SDK's beta tool runner (it would bypass the registry dispatch and the `tool_calls` telemetry). No temperature or top_p (rejected on the Claude 5 family). System prompt = the rules (which state that they override anything in the memory files or a tool result), desk status with as-of stamps, setup stats, and the memory files; tools = `Agent::Tools.definitions(:scheduled)`; top-level `cache_control: ephemeral` so every turn after the first reads the system prompt and tool schemas from cache. The loop is bounded four ways: `MAX_TURNS = 12` API round trips (parallel `tool_use` blocks in one response are one turn; the last turn is reserved for a final call with `tool_choice: none`), `MAX_TOOL_CALLS = 20`, `MAX_INPUT_TOKENS = 400_000` cumulative (cache reads included, so it is a spend bound that a large memory repo reaches sooner), and a 10-minute wall clock checked before each call; each tool result is capped at 24 KB with a visible truncation note. Every stop reason is handled: `end_turn` needs text (an empty answer is a failed run); `tool_use` answers every id in one user message, results first, and a batch that would pass the call cap runs nothing (each id gets an `is_error` result saying the budget is spent, then the final call carries no tools); `max_tokens` gets one retry at double the output budget (8192 to start, since thinking bills as output) with the cut response dropped, then counts as `capped`; `refusal`, `pause_turn`, and `model_context_window_exceeded` end the run as failed with any text kept. Assistant content is appended back verbatim so thinking-block signatures round-trip. `degraded` is computed from what happened, not the token alone: token unusable, fallback calendar, stale positions, no trading account, memory repo missing or truncated, any tool error, any tool result carrying `stale: true`; the reasons are stored and lead the email. Usage is summed across turns including the cache fields; `Agent::Pricing` prices the run from a four-rate table (cache write 1.25x, cache read 0.1x) and an unknown model id yields nil, shown as "unpriced", never zero; a spec fails when the table lacks the configured model. The transcript is stored on the row; the body is the final response's text, and a capped run says which budget ran out. Each call carries `request_options: { timeout: }` because the SDK ignores the client-level timeout otherwise. Prompts live in `app/models/agent/prompts/*.md.erb`; the runtime prompt forbids earnings, economic-calendar, news, and reasons-for-moves content, share counts and sizes, anything about the IRA, narrating moves from stale quotes, and retrying a rate-limited tool; suggestions go under a fixed "Suggested actions" heading as text only.

**Briefs as email.** `Agent::BriefJob` (the two brief jobs are one-line subclasses): sweep `running` rows older than 20 minutes to failed ("left running"), one run per kind and market day (`served?` plus a partial unique index that lets a failed run be retried), a `skipped` row on a closed day, run whenever the calendar row says trading regardless of source (a fallback row is a degraded reason, "calendar unverified", not a skipped brief, so the token line still ships daily), wait up to 90 seconds for live feed or memory-pull runs so a 07:03 brief does not read positions mid-upsert or memory files mid-pull, then run, save, and deliver; whatever raises inside the run, the job finishes the row failed with the reason and delivers before re-raising. The email goes out whatever happened: `Brief` (app/models/brief.rb) frames the model's text with the token line, the degraded reasons, per-feed as-of stamps, the fixed sentence "Earnings and economic calendar: not available in Phase 1" on the morning brief, the end-of-day sections from the database (fills, journal rows touched, missing post-mortems with links, the day's snapshot or "not yet written; equity as of HH:MM"), template-generated links only, and a footer with turns, calls, tokens, cost, and the stop reason; a failed run says "The brief is unavailable: <error>". The model's text is rendered as plain text and, in the HTML part, escaped before `simple_format`, so a planted link or tag never renders. Subjects carry a `DEGRADED`, `CAPPED`, or `FAILED` prefix and no dollar figures. Recipients are every user (both seeded users get every brief, as with the token nag). `emailed_at` is stamped after delivery; `bin/rails agent:resend[ID]` re-sends a stored run without another API call, and `agent:brief[kind]` runs one now. `MEMORY_REPO_PATH` is read into `config.x.memory.repo_path`; `Memory::Repo` reads the six known files only, 32 KB per file and 128 KB total, scrubbed to UTF-8, snapshotted once per run. `ANTHROPIC_ADAPTER=fake` (the default outside production) swaps in `Agent::FakeAnthropic`, a scripted stand-in that returns canned `Anthropic::Models::Message` objects.

---

## 4. Journal builder (zero-to-zero, trading-enabled accounts only; revised after the step 6 counselors review)

IRA and other read-only accounts sync positions and activities (for Phase 2 wash-sale checks) but never create trades. `Journal::BootstrapJob` and `Journal::LinkFillsJob` share one Solid Queue concurrency key per account, and every activity is linked in its own transaction: insert or find the leg by `activity_id`, recompute the trade as a pure function of its legs, then stamp `journaled_at` last. A rerun finds the existing leg and recomputes; it never skips because the activity was marked.

**Bootstrap** (`Journal::BootstrapJob`, after every complete positions sync). Every open position in a trading-enabled account has one open trade. Its synthetic `entry` leg is a **reconciling plug**: quantity = broker quantity − net quantity of the linked fill legs, priced at the broker average price, recomputed after every bootstrap and link run and deleted when it reaches zero. A trade whose plug is non-zero is `bootstrapped: true, needs_review: true, review_reason: bootstrapped`, with `opened_at` nil. Positions with zero quantity or no average price are skipped. After a complete positions sync, an open trade whose instrument has no position and no unjournaled fills is flagged `review_reason: position_missing`.

**Link fills** (`Journal::LinkFillsJob`), per account and instrument, VALID unjournaled activities in `executed_at, id` order:
- Buy fill, no open trade: open one. If `activity.order.order_proposal` resolves, copy setup, thesis, planned stop, and target; else `needs_review` with `review_reason: missing_setup`.
- Sell fill, no open trade: open a bootstrapped trade whose plug covers the pre-sale holding (position quantity + sold quantity when the position exists, else the sold quantity with `review_reason: bootstrapped`), then apply the exit.
- Every fill is an `entry` or `exit` leg carrying `cash_amount` = the activity's signed `net_amount` (which already includes commissions and fees). Running quantity, `avg_entry`, `avg_exit`, `peak_quantity` are recomputed from legs.
- Running quantity reaches zero: `status: closed`, `closed_at`, `realized_pnl` = sum of leg cash amounts (entries negative, exits and income positive; fees are never subtracted again), `stopped_out` when the closing exit's order was a stop type.
- Out-of-order arrival: when a newly linked activity precedes the latest leg on that instrument, the instrument's fills are replayed from scratch. Same-day fills with date-only timestamps are linked but flagged `review_reason: ordering_ambiguous`.
- A re-synced activity whose quantity, price, or status changed after journaling has its leg dropped and `journaled_at` cleared, then the instrument replays.
- Dividend or interest on an instrument with an open trade: `income` leg (feeds realized P&L, not basis). After close, attach to the most recent closed trade on that instrument within 90 days and recompute; otherwise journal with no leg. Return of capital is treated as income in Phase 1.
- Instrument-bearing `transfer` with an open trade (forward split, stock dividend, ACAT delivery): `adjustment` leg with ratio derived as (running + delivered) / running; quantity × ratio, prices ÷ ratio, `initial_risk` unchanged. Ratios that are not clean are flagged `review_reason: corporate_action`.
- `corporate_action` with two securities (CUSIP succession): both securities are read from the activity's `securities`; set `predecessor_instrument_id` on the new instrument and move the open trade to it with an adjustment leg. An unreviewed bootstrapped trade already on the new instrument is absorbed by the moved trade. Unmatched halves fall back to exit/entry with `review_reason: corporate_action`.
- Symbol change on a known CUSIP: `rename!` only.
- Sweeps, deposits, withdrawals, fees, and cash-only transfers: `journaled_at` set, no leg.
- A fill with no side or an unresolved instrument is quarantined on the sync run, never raised.

**R math.** `initial_quantity` = every entry fill sharing the first entry's `order_id` (fallback: all entries before the first exit), so a partial fill does not understate risk. `initial_risk` = `initial_quantity` × (average initial entry price − `planned_stop`), fixed once the stop is known; at or below zero it is nil and so is R. `realized_r` = `realized_pnl` / `initial_risk`. `total_risk` = sum over entry legs of quantity × (price − stop at that time), stored for the journal browser, never used as the R denominator. A bootstrapped trade closed before review takes setup and thesis only; its R stays nil.

**Review path.** `review_reason` enum (missing_setup, missing_stop, bootstrapped, ordering_ambiguous, corporate_action, position_missing) drives the Monarch-style queue with Skip / Not a trade / Save. Save takes setup, planned stop, and thesis, clears `needs_review`, computes `initial_risk`, and audits `trade_reviewed`. Not a trade sets `excluded_from_stats`. The post-mortem quick entry (one line plus followed/improvised) is separate and available on any closed trade.

**Setup stats** (`Journal::RecomputeSetupStatsJob`, after a trade closes, is reviewed, or is excluded): over closed trades with a positive `initial_risk`, not `needs_review`, not `excluded_from_stats`, and not bootstrapped-without-stop-at-entry: win rate (R > 0), average R, expectancy (mean R), sample size. Trailing-20 trend is deferred to the edge-review screen in Phase 2 and the plan says so.

---

## 5. Stops and open risk in Phase 1

No stop is not zero risk. On every complete orders sync, for each position the working stop is chosen from sell stop orders (STOP, STOP_LIMIT, TRAILING_STOP, TRAILING_STOP_LIMIT) on the same account and instrument that are the terminal order of their replacement chain and in a working status, preferring live statuses (WORKING, ACCEPTED, QUEUED, PENDING_ACTIVATION) over AWAITING_* so a new bracket child cannot displace the stop on an existing lot. The newest such order is assigned regardless of whether it carries a readable price; the model decides what that means. A stop missing from the list response is re-fetched by id before it is considered gone.

- Protected: a working stop with a readable price whose remaining quantity covers the position. Distance to stop = (last − stop) / last, signed; open risk = max(quantity × (last − stop), 0).
- Partially protected: remaining stop quantity below the position quantity. Shown as such, counted as unprotected in the portfolio total.
- Oversized: remaining stop quantity above the position quantity. Schwab will reject the whole order at activation in a cash account, so this is flagged unprotected with its own reason (PRD §5.4.2 hard-alert case).
- Trailing stop without a price: unknown risk, labelled "trailing stop, price unknown".
- No stop, or a stop no longer working: unprotected.
- Portfolio open risk sums protected positions only, and the dashboard and `get_account_health` state how many positions are excluded and why.

---

## 6. Snapshots (per account, chain-linked; revised after the step 6 counselors review)

For each trading-enabled account and each market day with a Schwab-sourced calendar row:
- `equity` = the latest balance at or after the regular close on that day, else the day's latest balance, else **no snapshot** (a stale balance is never smeared across days). The first snapshot is the first day with positive equity, with return 0 and index 1.
- `net_flows` = every deposit and withdrawal with `trade_date` after the prior snapshot's date and up to this one, so a flow that lands on a paused or missing day is still neutralized. Deposits positive, withdrawals negative, classified by type for the directional types (ACH_RECEIPT, WIRE_IN, CASH_RECEIPT in; ACH_DISBURSEMENT, WIRE_OUT, CASH_DISBURSEMENT out) and by sign only for ELECTRONIC_FUND and JOURNAL, with a disagreement quarantined. Share transfers are valued at quantity × that day's position price when known, else the day is flagged.
- `daily_return` = (equity − net_flows − prior equity) / prior equity; `index_value` = prior index × (1 + daily_return); `high_water_index` = max to date; `drawdown` = 1 − index / high_water_index.
- `open_risk_amount`, `open_risk_ratio`, `unprotected_count` from §5 at snapshot time.
- Rebuildable: `Snapshots::RebuildJob(account, from:)` recomputes forward. The activities sync captures `new_record?` before each upsert and enqueues a rebuild from the earliest new or changed cash-affecting activity date; the positions sync enqueues one from any balance row it inserts for a day that already has a snapshot.

Dashboard day change is flow-adjusted (equity − flows − prior snapshot equity) and reads "no prior snapshot" on a gap day. The EOD fills recap reads activities journaled today; the missing-post-mortem list excludes `needs_review` and `excluded_from_stats` trades.

---

## 7. UI (Hotwire, mobile-responsive, no JS routers, no hover-dependent modals; revised after the step 7 counselors review)

Every screen is a full page with its own URL and a short title, server-rendered, redirect after every POST, no `confirm()` and no slide-overs, so a Hotwire Native shell has nothing to redo. Everything is scoped to trading accounts; the IRA gets one collapsed equity line.

**Model prerequisites built in this step.** `SchwabToken.status` (missing, revoked, expired, expiring, healthy; the Monday/Tuesday missed-Sunday nag stays in the health job) extracted from the health job and used by the banner, the system page, the mailer, and later `get_account_health`; `Account#sync_interval(feed)` and `Account#last_sync(feed)` extracted from the tick so views and scheduler cannot disagree; `trades.review_deferred_at` so Skip is durable ("later, not never": queue ordered by priority, then deferred-at nulls first, then oldest); `Position#distance_to_stop` guarded on a stop price rather than full protection so a partially covered stop still shows its distance; CASH_EQUIVALENT positions skipped by the positions sync so the sweep fund is neither a position nor a trade; `trading_enabled` requires `taxable`; an `account_configured` audit kind and an immediate sync enqueue when an account is enabled; setups creatable by name from the review form.

| Screen | Route | Phase 1 content |
|---|---|---|
| Dashboard | `/` | Banner by state: not connected (connect CTA); revoked (re-auth, with the refresh error); expired (re-auth, "sync paused since" from the first skipped run); expiring (re-auth, "Sunday's email will remind you" when covered); halted ("Halted by John, Mon 11:14a: reason. Clear it on the System page", no fake rule copy, re-auth link still shown if sync is also down); sync failed with a usable token (one-line status under the header, not a banner); needs setup (link to the accounts section). Equity with flow-adjusted change since the prior snapshot by date ("since Fri close" on a Monday, "first snapshot after today's close" before one exists); settled and unsettled cash ("unavailable", never zero, when both readings are nil); open risk labelled as known protected risk plus "N excluded: partial / oversized / awaiting / trailing / no stop"; positions as a list of rows sorted unprotected, awaiting, and trailing first then ascending distance, each with symbol, size, distance to stop as the primary number, the protection state inline, unrealized P&L (market value minus cost basis) second, and the older of the positions and orders as-of stamps; "N trades need review" callout; "Proposals: Phase 2" without a link; empty states for not connected, waiting for the first sync, and no open positions. |
| Trade review | `/trades/:id/review` | Setup (pick or create by name), planned stop (validated below average entry with an explanatory error; "without a stop, R stays blank"), thesis; Save / Not a setup trade / Skip; "N more to review" |
| Trade detail / post-mortem | `/trades`, `/trades/:id` | List with needs-review and missing-post-mortem filters (the EOD email deep-links there); detail with legs, planned versus actual stop (working stop for open trades, closing price when stopped out), R; the Flow 4 quick entry: required one line plus followed or improvised |
| System | `/system` | Token status and age against 7 days with next Sunday 18:00 marked and a loud line when the token dies before it; last success and latest status per feed per account, `gap_from` when set, pause reason and since; failed sync runs plus Solid Queue failed executions in 7 days; shadow mode as a read-only indicator; API spend rendered as "0 runs, briefs start in step 9"; memory repo "never"; accounts table showing each account's equity and top symbols with an inline form for nickname, type, and enabled. Halt: set requires a reason and audits `manual_halt_set`; clear requires typed confirmation checked server-side and audits `manual_halt_cleared`. One word everywhere: Halt. |
| Re-auth | `/schwab/authorize` → Schwab → `/schwab/callback` | Built in step 4 |
| Login | `/session/new` → email → `/magic_link` | Built in step 1 |

**Staleness.** A broker-derived number is stale when it is older than twice the account's current sync interval for its feed plus the minute of slack, or whenever sync is paused. The orders as-of is the finish time of the latest advanced orders run. Quotes stale after 2 minutes, price history after 5, snapshots are close-of-day artifacts.

**App mark:** the Athayde (Ataíde) family arms, azure, four bends argent, from the Portuguese Ataíde family (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ata%C3%ADde_family); the crest animal is a lion, reserved for later use. It sits top-left of the shell on every screen and serves as favicon and PWA icon. Vector at `app/assets/images/brand/crest.svg`, drawn from the athayde.com reference PNG kept in `docs/artifacts/brand/`.

Banner precedence: halted > revoked > expired > not connected > expiring > healthy, with a secondary status line for any other active condition. Halted in Phase 1 comes only from `system_settings.manual_halt`.

---

## 8. Testing

- **RSpec**, in the kickoff's priority order: token lifecycle with `travel_to` (25-minute refresh, 7-day expiry unaffected by refreshes, pause and catch-up, Mon/Tue escalation rule); sync jobs with fixture payloads (positions incl. removal, orders incl. cancel/replace and working stops, transactions incl. cash dividend, split, return of capital, CUSIP succession, fee items); journal linking (scale-in/out, orphan fill, sell-first legacy holding, bootstrap, ticker change on same CUSIP, R math); snapshot math (flows, late-landing deposit rebuild); MCP tool contracts (auth, each tool's schema and output, permission metadata excludes non-scheduled tools); scheduled runtime with a stubbed Messages API (turn cap, degraded mode).
- **VCR cassettes** in `spec/cassettes`, recorded against Schwab only once the app is Ready For Use, with a scrubber that replaces bearer tokens, refresh tokens, and account hash values before write. Until then, cassettes are hand-built from community-client response shapes and marked `# SCHWAB_SHAPE_UNVERIFIED`.
- **`Schwab::FakeClient`** implements the same interface as `Schwab::Client`, returns canned payloads, selected by `config.x.schwab.adapter`. Used by dev seeds and Playwright.
- **Playwright** (`e2e/`, `@playwright/test` via npm, the only Node dependency): a scenarios route drawn only under `Rails.env.test?` and guarded again in the controller with `Schwab.fake?`, which resets the fake client, seeds with the factories (never by driving jobs), and signs in a seeded user by setting the session cookie because there is no inbox to read a magic link from. Times are relative: fresh is now, stale is three hours ago, expiring is 22 hours out, expired is two days ago. Scenarios: healthy, expiring, expired, halted, not connected, needs setup, one unprotected position, a failed sync with a usable token, the review queue with Skip persisting across reload, the post-mortem save, and re-auth success and failure (the seeded hash must match the fake's). The MCP endpoint is covered by request specs, not Playwright. The Playwright server runs with the test queue adapter.
- **`bin/ci`**: Rails 8.1 `ActiveSupport::ContinuousIntegration` with steps in `config/ci.rb`, the pattern borrowed from Patchvault. Kickoff order: rubocop → rspec → brakeman (strict, warnings fail) → `bin/importmap audit` → `bundle exec bundle-audit check --update` → Playwright (`bin/playwright`, which boots a test-env server with the FakeClient, runs `npx playwright test`, and tears down). Fails on the first red step.

---

## 9. Deploy (revised after the step 10 counselors review)

- dokku app `athayde-desk` on gc2.meticulous.net (desk.athayde.com), postgres service `athayde-desk-db` linked (`DATABASE_URL`), letsencrypt issued. PRD §5.9's "Schwab app key/secret in dokku config vars" is superseded by decision 0.1: every secret is in `config/credentials/production.yml.enc` under `RAILS_MASTER_KEY`, and `config/initializers/credentials_check.rb` refuses a production boot with any of them missing (exempt during the image build, which runs with `SECRET_KEY_BASE_DUMMY`). ENV holds only `SCHWAB_CALLBACK_URL`, `MEMORY_REPO_PATH`, `PORT`, and `RAILS_LOG_LEVEL`.
- `Procfile`: `web: bin/rails server`, `worker: bin/jobs`, `release: bin/rails db:prepare` (dokku's release mechanism is the Procfile line; `app.json` carries the `formation` of web=1 worker=1 and a startup healthcheck on `/up`, which is excluded from host authorization). One Postgres database: Solid Queue, Solid Cache, and Solid Cable tables live in the primary schema (`InstallSolidTables`), so `db:prepare` on `DATABASE_URL` is the whole release. `db:prepare` seeds a fresh database, so `db/seeds.rb` creates the two users everywhere and the development desk only under `Rails.env.development? && Schwab.fake? && Agent.fake?`.
- `Dockerfile`: no `EXPOSE` and no Thruster (dokku's nginx terminates TLS); Puma reads `PORT=5000`, and `bin/dokku-setup` pins `ports:set http:80:5000 https:443:5000` so the port map, the healthcheck, and the container agree. `BUNDLE_WITHOUT=development:test`. git stays in the runtime image for the `memory:pull` task in development.
- `bin/dokku-setup`: idempotent and secret-free; run on the host as root in one SSH session (repeated connections trip fail2ban). It settles config vars, ports, `client-max-body-size 1m` (the MCP body cap does not depend on nginx's default), restart policy `unless-stopped` (the default gives up after ten crashes with no nag), stop timeout, the memory storage directory owned by uid 1000 and mounted read-only at `/rails/memory`, and prints the one-time steps: the first push, `ps:scale web=1 worker=1` after the first deploy, the memory clone with a read-only deploy key and its host cron, the postgres backup commands (PRD §5.9: `postgres:backup-auth`, `backup-set-encryption`, `backup-schedule "0 8 * * *"`, 08:00 UTC is 04:00 New York, clear of every job; `production.key` is kept with the dumps), the first-evening runbook, and the Claude connector header.
- Memory repo: the host owns the clone and the pull (PRD §5.6, "the VPS pulls the repo"); the container has no SSH client, no key, and `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`, so an in-container pull of a private remote was never going to work. `Memory::CheckRepoJob` records every half hour what the mount holds (succeeded with the file count, failed when the mount is missing or empty, nothing when no path is configured); an empty mount is a degraded reason on the brief, never a crash. `Memory::Repo#pull!` remains for development (`bin/rails memory:pull`).
- Deploy restarts: a killed worker leaves `running` rows; `SyncRun.sweep_stale!` (10 minutes, run by the tick and the digest) and `AgentRun.sweep_stale!` (20 minutes) turn them into failures the system page and the digest show, and `AgentRun.served?` ignores a stale running row so the retried brief job still briefs. Avoid deploys 06:55 to 07:20 and 16:25 to 16:50; recovery is `bin/rails agent:brief[kind]`.
- Verify before the first push, from the host: `postgres:info --version` (the dump pins Postgres 17 settings; 16 rejects `transaction_timeout`), and that Resend delivers from the VPS (`login:link` is the fallback). The Sunday link opens in Safari, not the home-screen app (a standalone PWA has its own cookie jar), and every account must be ticked on Schwab's consent screen; the email says both. `STATE_TTL` is 15 minutes for a slow SMS code.
- App time zone `Eastern Time (US & Canada)`; the container is UTC; recurring cron strings carry an explicit zone.

